 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "8lmiEQa6AgAJ",
   "from": "Guilherme Parreira",
   "author": "6667ef04b386b9ec",
   "date": "2020-04-13T12:23:54Z",
   "subject": "How to include a different probability distribution in TMB in R?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p>I am using the TMB package in R, and I want to use a density distribution which is not available in the <a href=\"https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/group__R__style__distribution.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/group__R__style__distribution.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183232000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1qdI2NIoIXBjd49KNRNjnm\">R style distribution</a>. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>How should I include it? I found something at <a href=\"https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/wiki/Code--snippets\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/wiki/Code--snippets&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183232000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0yC8DF6rxSpe2OHFUw3pb5\">Code--snippets</a>, but as I am new to it, I am not quite sure.<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>For example, I want to use Simplex distribution, which I have already written in R:</p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">dsimplex </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> dis </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> dsim </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">/(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dis<br> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">if</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">==</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dsim </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dsim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dsim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br><p>and it is available in <code>simplexreg</code> and <code>Stan</code> packages.<br><br></p><p>What is the best approach to deal with it? (I would like to have an \r\nexternal file which contains this density, and call it from the file \r\nthat I am writing my log likelihood).<br>Thanks in advance!!<br></p></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "5FMhCZOBAgAJ",
   "from": "Mollie Brooks",
   "author": "55d0e2f87b0f795e",
   "date": "2020-04-15T13:23:34Z",
   "subject": "Re: [TMB users] How to include a different probability distribution in TMB in R?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">There are a couple of examples of this (e.g. dgenpois, dbetabinom) in the glmmTMB code here <div><a href=\"https://github.com/glmmTMB/glmmTMB/blob/master/glmmTMB/src/glmmTMB.cpp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/glmmTMB/glmmTMB/blob/master/glmmTMB/src/glmmTMB.cpp&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183235000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3x9x_85Bh4aMyiezj9AELe\">https://github.com/glmmTMB/glmmTMB/blob/master/glmmTMB/src/glmmTMB.cpp</a></div><div><br></div><div>I think you would skip line 7 referring to the namespace of glmmTMB and simply structure the definition the same as in the posfun example at the Code-snippets link.</div><div><br></div><div>In the example, the definition is after the include statement</div><div>#include &lt;TMB.hpp&gt;</div><div><br></div><div>, but before the objective function definition</div><div><div>template&lt;class Type&gt;</div><div>Type objective_function&lt;Type&gt;::operator() ()</div><div>{</div></div><div><br></div><div>Maybe someone else can comment on your request for doing this in an external file. That’s beyond my working memory of C++.</div><div><br></div><div>cheers,</div><div>Mollie<br><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On 13Apr 2020, at 14:23, Guilherme Parreira &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guilhermepa...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><p>I am using the TMB package in R, and I want to use a density distribution which is not available in the <a href=\"https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/group__R__style__distribution.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/group__R__style__distribution.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183235000&amp;usg=AOvVaw336aPQZJhpm7lT1cg9NAQg\">R style distribution</a>. </p><p>How should I include it? I found something at <a href=\"https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/wiki/Code--snippets\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/wiki/Code--snippets&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183235000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2kTfc7omYarThJCqXu5lLk\">Code--snippets</a>, but as I am new to it, I am not quite sure.<br></p><p>For example, I want to use Simplex distribution, which I have already written in R:</p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span>dsimplex </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span>TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span><br> dis </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)*</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br> dsim </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>pi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span><br> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)*</span><span>log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">/(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span>sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))*</span><span>dis<br> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">if</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">==</span><span> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span>dsim </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>dsim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)}</span><span><br> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>dsim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br></span></div></code></div><br><p>and it is available in <code>simplexreg</code> and <code>Stan</code> packages.<br><br></p><p>What is the best approach to deal with it? (I would like to have an \r\nexternal file which contains this density, and call it from the file \r\nthat I am writing my log likelihood).<br>Thanks in advance!!<br></p></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">us...@tmb-project.org</a>. Before posting, please check the wiki and issuetracker at <a href=\"https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183235000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0b1UnGBSgTRhAsFwdczZ8x\">https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/</a>. Please try to create a simple repeatable example to go with your question (e.g issues 154, 134, 51). Use the issuetracker to report bugs.<br>\r\n--- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;TMB Users&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tmb-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/tmb-users/580b1c70-1ebd-41a9-8dc3-76b1beb30142%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/tmb-users/580b1c70-1ebd-41a9-8dc3-76b1beb30142%2540googlegroups.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183235000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1itbTxsY3pto9tAzHqykDq\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/tmb-users/580b1c70-1ebd-41a9-8dc3-76b1beb30142%40googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\n</div></blockquote></div><br></div></div>"
