   "subject": "Re: [TMB users] How to include a different probability distribution in TMB in R?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Guilherme and Mollie,</div><div><br></div><div>If you want to include helper functions, like a new probability distribution, in an external file (e.g. &quot;helper_functions.hpp&quot;), you just need to #include it at the top of your .cpp between the call to TMB.hpp and your objective function. Our <a href=\"https://github.com/timjmiller/wham/tree/master/src\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/timjmiller/wham/tree/master/src&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183239000&amp;usg=AOvVaw29_VwMX9zEAzWLAzpr2N6q\">WHAM package</a> has a few examples where we store several probability distributions and other functions in &quot;helper_functions.hpp&quot;.</div><div><br></div><div>#include &lt;TMB.hpp&gt;<br>#include &quot;helper_functions.hpp&quot;</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>template&lt;class Type&gt;<br>Type objective_function&lt;Type&gt;::operator() ()<br>{</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div></div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Brian<br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wed, Apr 15, 2020 at 9:23 AM Mollie Brooks &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mollie...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>There are a couple of examples of this (e.g. dgenpois, dbetabinom) in the glmmTMB code here <div><a href=\"https://github.com/glmmTMB/glmmTMB/blob/master/glmmTMB/src/glmmTMB.cpp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/glmmTMB/glmmTMB/blob/master/glmmTMB/src/glmmTMB.cpp&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183239000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2E0ukRfpBgRgGrVweAL5wt\">https://github.com/glmmTMB/glmmTMB/blob/master/glmmTMB/src/glmmTMB.cpp</a></div><div><br></div><div>I think you would skip line 7 referring to the namespace of glmmTMB and simply structure the definition the same as in the posfun example at the Code-snippets link.</div><div><br></div><div>In the example, the definition is after the include statement</div><div>#include &lt;TMB.hpp&gt;</div><div><br></div><div>, but before the objective function definition</div><div><div>template&lt;class Type&gt;</div><div>Type objective_function&lt;Type&gt;::operator() ()</div><div>{</div></div><div><br></div><div>Maybe someone else can comment on your request for doing this in an external file. That’s beyond my working memory of C++.</div><div><br></div><div>cheers,</div><div>Mollie<br><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On 13Apr 2020, at 14:23, Guilherme Parreira &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guilhermepa...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><p>I am using the TMB package in R, and I want to use a density distribution which is not available in the <a href=\"https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/group__R__style__distribution.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/group__R__style__distribution.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183239000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Ffl1WbToMs6WTLvGv7fjP\">R style distribution</a>. </p><p>How should I include it? I found something at <a href=\"https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/wiki/Code--snippets\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/wiki/Code--snippets&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183239000&amp;usg=AOvVaw23QJqSQndit-Q3rzxyvHyf\">Code--snippets</a>, but as I am new to it, I am not quite sure.<br></p><p>For example, I want to use Simplex distribution, which I have already written in R:</p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span>dsimplex </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">function</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span> mu</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span> sigma</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span> log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span>TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">){</span><span><br> dis </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)^</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">/</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)*</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">^</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)^</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span><br> dsim </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span>log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span>pi</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span>log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>sigma</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span><br> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">3</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">/</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)*</span><span>log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span>y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">/(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span>sigma</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))*</span><span>dis<br> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">if</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>log </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">==</span><span> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">){</span><span>dsim </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span> exp</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>dsim</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)}</span><span><br> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">return</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span>dsim</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">}</span><span><br></span></div></code></div><br><p>and it is available in <code>simplexreg</code> and <code>Stan</code> packages.<br><br></p><p>What is the best approach to deal with it? (I would like to have an \r\nexternal file which contains this density, and call it from the file \r\nthat I am writing my log likelihood).<br>Thanks in advance!!<br></p></div><div><br></div>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">us...@tmb-project.org</a>. Before posting, please check the wiki and issuetracker at <a href=\"https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462183239000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1v2uj84T98Cp7QI1NVgPjL\">https://github.com/kaskr/adcomp/</a>. Please try to create a simple repeatable example to go with your question (e.g issues 154, 134, 51). Use the issuetracker to report bugs.<br>\r\n--- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;TMB Users&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tmb-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/tmb-users/580b1c70-1ebd-41a9-8dc3-76b1beb30142%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=